\begin_layout Section
Real runnings = real results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this chapter I will show the results obtained with the real runnings of my scripts in the IBM Quantum Platform.
 An important consideration that have to be made is that in some case the results will be really different from the simulations because sometimes the real hardware can't give us some data like the full state of a vector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A real QPU can’t give the full state because every measurement destroys it.
 It also gives only one bit per qubit each time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The real hardware make a measurement for each run of the circuit.
 The measurement forces each qubit to a definite 0 or 1 and 
\bar under
erases the superposition
\bar default
.
 You get a string like “01” and nothing else.
 The amplitudes (the complex numbers in front of |00⟩,
 |01⟩ and so on) are never read out directly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The simulator is a program that stores the whole state vector in memory,
 so it can simply print it.
 A physical device has no such memory.
 The state exists only in the physics of the qubits,
 and you cannot copy it (the 
\emph on
no-cloning theorem
\emph default
) or look at it without disturbing it.
\end_layout

