\shape italic
two independent qubits with QPU
\shape default
]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
hspace{1mm}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure X shows the result window of experiment 1 when the circuit is executed on a real IBM quantum processor instead of the ideal simulator.
 The title box reports the two prepared states,
 qubit A with angle 0.43 rad and qubit B with angle 2.00 rad,
 which are antipodal by construction.
 The second line reports the entanglement witness,
 defined as the sum of the absolute values of the three two-qubit correlators,
 together with the noise index.
 The third line states that the joint state amplitudes are not available on real hardware,
 because a physical device returns only measurement statistics and not the underlying state vector.
 
\emph on
This is the main difference from the simulator version of the same window,
 where the amplitudes of the four basis states are printed explicitly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The measured witness is 1.07.
 For two independent qubits prepared as antipodal states,
 the ideal value computed from the Bloch vectors is 1.00 (⟨XX⟩ ≈ −0.57,
 ⟨YY⟩ = 0,
 ⟨ZZ⟩ ≈ −0.43),
 and 1 is also the upper bound for any separable state.
 The measured value exceeds this bound by only 0.07,
 a deviation compatible with statistical fluctuation over a finite number of shots and with readout errors.
 
\emph on
It cannot be interpreted as entanglement,
 since the circuit contains no entangling gate.

\emph default
 The noise index is 1.7%,
 which corresponds to an average distance of about 0.03 between the measured and the ideal Bloch vectors,
 on a scale where the maximum possible distance is 2.
 The deviation from the ideal simulation is therefore small.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two Bloch spheres show vectors of length 0.99 for both qubits.
 The simulator gives exactly 1.00,
 because the states are pure.
 
\emph on
The slight reduction is the direct signature of hardware noise,
 since decoherence and gate errors turn a pure state into a slightly mixed one,
 which moves the vector inside the sphere.

\emph default
 The two arrows remain approximately opposite to each other,
 as required by the antipodal construction.
 The probability chart shows P(|0⟩) = 0.82 and P(|1⟩) = 0.18 for qubit 0,
 and the mirrored values for qubit 1.
 The ideal values for these angles are cos²(0.43) ≈ 0.82 and sin²(0.43) ≈ 0.18,
 so the agreement is within the displayed precision.
 As in the simulator,
 the two probability pairs are exact mirror images of each other,
 which confirms that the qubits behave independently.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In conclusion,
 the real processor reproduces the behavior of the simulator for experiment 1.
 The vectors lose only about 1% of their length,
 the probabilities are essentially unchanged,
 and the witness stays close to the separable bound.
 This experiment can therefore serve as a baseline for estimating the intrinsic noise of the device.
 It is the reference against which the larger deviations of the entangled experiments can be judged.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Experiment 2:
 partially entangled vectors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ../img/exp2_ibm_visualization1.png
	lyxscale 30
	scale 30

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align right
[
\family typewriter
exp2_ibm_visualization1.png
\family default
:
 
\emph on
two partially entangled qubits with QPU
\emph default
]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The figure shows the result window of experiment 2 when the circuit is executed on a real IBM quantum processor.
 The title box reports the rotation angle assigned to the matched emoji,
 θ = 1.08 rad,
 and the corresponding state before the sign encoding,
 cos(0.54)|00⟩ + sin(0.54)|11⟩.
 The second line reports the entanglement witness,
 defined as the sum of the absolute values of the three two-qubit correlators,
 together with the noise index.
 The third line states that the joint amplitudes are not available on real hardware,
 since only measurement statistics can be obtained from a physical device.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The measured witness is 2.72.
 The ideal value for this angle is 1 + 2 sin θ ≈ 2.76,
 so the hardware reproduces the expected correlations to within about 1.5%.
 Since the bound for separable states is 1,
 
\emph on
the measured value is a clear experimental indication that the two qubits are entangled,
 and the entanglement survives the noise of the device.

\emph default
 The noise index is 3.8%,
 corresponding to an average distance of about 0.08 between the measured and ideal Bloch vectors.
 This is higher than the 1.7% obtained in experiment 1,
 which is consistent with the presence of a two-qubit gate.
 Two-qubit gates are in general the main source of error in current processors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two Bloch spheres show vectors of length 0.51 and 0.50,
 pointing in opposite directions along the vertical axis.
 Their short length is the expected visual signature of entanglement.
 
\emph on
When a qubit is part of an entangled pair,
 its individual state is mixed,
 and its vector shrinks inside the sphere.

\emph default
 The ideal length for this angle is cos θ ≈ 0.47,
 so the measured vectors are slightly longer.
 
\emph on
This small difference is compatible with the noise,
 which also modifies the reduced states.

\emph default
 The arrow of qubit 1 is slightly tilted away from the vertical axis.
 This is a small transverse component that is absent in the ideal case and is attributable to phase errors on the hardware.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The joint probability chart shows P(01) = 0.71 and P(10) = 0.24,
 against the ideal values of 0.74 and 0.26.
 In addition,
 the outcomes 00 and 11,
 which are forbidden in the ideal circuit,
 appear with probabilities 0.01 and 0.04.
 Together they account for about 5% of the shots,
 which is a direct measure of the errors of the entangling gate and of the readout.
 The dominant outcome and the asymmetry between 01 and 10 are the same as in the simulator,
 where the corresponding figure also showed one strongly dominant outcome and one weaker outcome.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In conclusion,
 the real processor preserves the main features of the partially entangled state of experiment 2.
 These are the reduced Bloch vectors,
 the asymmetric probabilities and a witness well above the separable bound.
 The deviations are small and consistent with the noise index.
 
\emph on
The most visible effects are the leakage of about 5% of the probability into the forbidden outcomes and the slight lengthening of the vectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Experiment 3:
 Bell states and density matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ../img/exp3_ibm_visualization1.png
	lyxscale 30
	scale 30

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
