\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two points before the complete explanation of the image:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bell state:
 this run is Ψ⁺,
 while the simulator figure I described earlier showed Ψ⁻ (see the picture in chapter 3.3.3 with name 
\family typewriter
exp3_ch3_visualization1.png
\family default
).
 The card is chosen by the game,
 so the Bell state varies between runs.
 Both states have the same probability support (01 and 10),
 so the comparison is direct.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Left panel:
 
\bar under
on hardware the window shows the two Bloch spheres where the simulator shows the density matrix grid
\bar default
.
 
\emph on
The density matrix needs the full state vector,
 that a real device does not provide.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The ideal values for Ψ⁺ are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Witness:
 3.0,
 from ⟨XX⟩ = +1,
 ⟨YY⟩ = +1 and ⟨ZZ⟩ = −1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bloch vectors:
 length 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Probabilities:
 0.5 for 01 and 0.5 for 10.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Because the ideal vectors have zero length,
 the noise index here is just the average measured vector length.
 A 3.7% index corresponds to a length of about 0.07,
 which matches the tiny arrows in the figure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The figure shows the result window of experiment 3 when the circuit is executed on a real IBM quantum processor.
 The title box reports the Bell state produced for the matched card,
 Ψ⁺ = (|01⟩ + |10⟩)/√2,
 followed by the entanglement witness and the noise index.
 The witness is defined as the sum of the absolute values of the three two-qubit correlators ⟨XX⟩,
 ⟨YY⟩ and ⟨ZZ⟩.
 
\emph on
A third line states that the joint amplitudes are not available on real hardware.
 For this reason the density matrix panel of the simulator version is replaced here by the single-qubit Bloch spheres,
 which can be reconstructed from measurement statistics alone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The measured witness is 2.90,
 against the ideal value of 3.0 for a maximally entangled Bell state.
 The hardware therefore recovers about 97% of the ideal correlation strength.
 The separable bound for this quantity is 1,
 so the measured value lies far above it and certifies the entanglement of the pair.
 The noise index is 3.7%,
 a value similar to the 3.8% obtained in experiment 2.
 This indicates that the main contribution to the error comes from the entangling gate,
 and that it does not depend on the particular state being produced.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two Bloch spheres show very short arrows close to the centre.
 For a maximally entangled pair the reduced state of each qubit is completely mixed,
 so the ideal Bloch vector has length zero,
 as in the simulator.
 
\emph on
The residual length,
 about 0.07 according to the noise index,
 is the visible effect of the hardware noise.

\emph default
 A pure single-qubit state would be represented by an arrow reaching the surface of the sphere,
 so the contrast between the two situations makes the presence of entanglement visually evident.
 The small arrows are also slightly displaced from the centre,
 which reflects a weak residual polarization of each qubit that is absent in the ideal state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The joint probability chart shows P(01) = 0.50 and P(10) = 0.49,
 with P(00) = 0.00 and P(11) = 0.01.
 The ideal values are 0.5 for each of the two correlated outcomes and 0 for the others.
 The two outcomes are balanced,
 as in the simulator figure,
 and the probability leaking into the forbidden outcomes is only about 1%.
 The result also illustrates the central property of the state.
 
\emph on
Each qubit separately gives a random result,
 while the pair always gives opposite values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
In conclusion,
 the real processor produces the Bell state Ψ⁺ with high fidelity.

\emph default
 The witness of 2.90 out of 3.0,
 the nearly maximal mixing of the single-qubit states and the balanced correlated probabilities agree with the simulation.
 The only visible deviations are small,
 namely the short residual arrows and the 1% probability in the outcome 11.
\end_layout

