\begin_layout Standard
The figure shows the result window of experiment 5 when the circuit is executed on a real IBM quantum processor.
 The title box reports the state produced for the matched card,
 (|00⟩ − |01⟩ + |10⟩ + |11⟩)/2,
 followed by the witness and the noise index.
 A third line states that the joint amplitudes are unavailable on real hardware.
 In the simulator version,
 the same state is shown through a density matrix grid in which every cell is populated,
 with a characteristic pattern of signs.
 That panel cannot be reconstructed from measurement statistics alone and is therefore replaced on hardware by the single-qubit Bloch spheres.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This experiment differs from the previous ones in an important respect.
 
\emph on
The state is entangled only through its phases.
 The probabilities of the four outcomes are all equal to 0.25,
 so a measurement in the computational basis gives no sign of correlation.

\emph default
 The entanglement is revealed only by the correlators on the right.
 The ideal values are ⟨XX⟩ = 0,
 ⟨YY⟩ = −1 and ⟨ZZ⟩ = 0,
 and the measured values are 0.04,
 −0.96 and 0.10.
 The dominant correlator is therefore recovered with an error of only 4%,
 and the two correlators that should vanish stay below 0.10.
 The negative sign of ⟨YY⟩ is reproduced correctly,
 which shows that the hardware preserves the phase structure of the state and not only the populations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The witness,
 defined as the sum of the absolute values of the three correlators,
 is 1.10.
 Unlike in experiments 2 to 4,
 this quantity cannot be used here to certify entanglement.
 For this state the ideal value is 1.0,
 which coincides with the separable bound,
 because only one correlator is different from zero.
 The value 1.10 is therefore compatible with the ideal one,
 and the small excess comes from the residual values of ⟨XX⟩ and ⟨ZZ⟩.
 
\emph on
The evidence for entanglement comes instead from the single-qubit vectors.

\emph default
 Their length is 0.07 for both qubits,
 against an ideal value of exactly zero.
 For a pure global state,
 completely mixed single-qubit states imply maximal entanglement.
 The near-zero length together with the strong ⟨YY⟩ correlation identifies a state that is highly entangled,
 even though the witness cannot show it.
 The noise index of 3.5% corresponds to this residual length of about 0.07,
 and is similar to the values of 3.7% and 3.8% found for experiments 3 and 2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In conclusion,
 the real processor reproduces the phase-entangled state of experiment 5 with good accuracy.
 The correlators are within 0.10 of their ideal values,
 the vectors are almost at the centre of the spheres,
 and the sign structure is preserved.
 
\emph on
The experiment also shows a limit of the witness,
 which for this class of states stays at the separable bound even though the entanglement is maximal.
\end_layout

